# EXEMPTIONS FOR THE PUBLICATION COMPLETENESS CONTRACT.
#
# `.publication-boundary` declares that this repository gets published, and it
# switches on BOTH contracts: the leak guards (may this leave?) and
# richos/engine/scripts/publication-completeness.sh (is everything that must be there,
# there, and usable by someone who has only the public repo?). This file adds
# no adoption switch and no thresholds. It carries deliberate, committed,
# reviewable exemptions and nothing else.
#
# WHY AN ESCAPE HATCH AT ALL
# --------------------------
# The same reason the boundary has an ALLOWLIST and no override token: a check
# with no sanctioned way through gets removed whole, and then protects nothing.
# So the way through is a line in a committed file, reviewed in a diff, with a
# reason written next to it — never an in-the-moment judgement call, which is
# the exact thing both contracts exist to replace.
#
# WHY THAT IS SAFE
# ----------------
#   AN ENTRY THAT SUPPRESSES NOTHING IS A FAILURE.
#
# Every exemption list is a place drift hides: the line was written for a real
# reason, the reason was fixed, and the line stayed — silently licensing the
# next instance of the defect. So the checker FAILS on an entry that no longer
# suppresses anything and names it for deletion. An exemption here cannot
# outlive its own justification.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CITATION_EXEMPT — documents whose paths describe A DIFFERENT REPOSITORY.
#
# Check 1 requires every path a public document cites to resolve in the public
# tree. That is right for a document describing THIS repository and wrong for a
# document describing the adopter's. These four trees are the latter, and each
# is template material by construction rather than by opinion:
#
#   richos/engine/reference/      Its own README already says so, in prose: "Cross-file
#                          path references still point at the original layout and
#                          will not resolve here — that is expected for reference
#                          material." This line makes that existing declaration
#                          machine-readable; it grants no new license.
#   richos/engine/skills/         Skills instruct an agent working in the ADOPTER's repo
#                          (`scripts/railway-api.sh`), name files a skill's own
#                          run creates (`.claude/product-marketing-context.md`,
#                          `ceo-wiki/wiki/vision-and-positioning.md`), and cite
#                          UPSTREAM provenance for vendored skills
#                          (`skills/taste-skill/SKILL.md` @ 72e2995).
#   richos/engine/.claude/agents/ Agent definitions are the adopter's team. They name
#                          the adopter's own entry points (`scripts/preflight.sh`).
#   richos/engine/qa-audits/      EXAMPLE_AUDIT.md is a shape to copy; its evidence
#                          screenshots are the adopter's, taken during their audit.
#
# THE COST, stated rather than discovered: a genuinely dangling citation inside
# one of these four trees is not caught. That is the price of the precision —
# without these, the check reported 26 findings of which 16 were correct
# documents doing their job, and a check that is 60% noise gets switched off.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CITATION_EXEMPT, second group — FROZEN RECORDS OF CODE THAT WAS LATER REMOVED.
#
# Added 2026-09-09, after the orchestration rollback (`8397c530`, recorded in
# `docs/verification/owned-outcome/ROLLBACK-2026-09-09.md`) deleted the managed
# assignment controller, registrar, worker, inspector, desktop commands and
# assignment panel. Twenty-three citations across the eight files below went
# dangling in one commit, and every commit to this repository was refused from
# that moment — the rollback removed the code and left the record pointing at it.
#
# THESE CITATIONS WERE TRUE WHEN THEY WERE WRITTEN. They are reviews, signoffs
# and revision records of software that existed, was reviewed, was signed off and
# was then deliberately removed. The rollback record says so in its own words:
# "Historical reviews, signoffs and evidence remain in Git." Rewriting a signoff
# to cite something else would falsify the one kind of document whose whole value
# is that it says what was true on the day. Restoring the code to satisfy a
# citation would be worse. So the honest form is this: the documents stand
# unedited, and the exemption states why.
#
# EXACT FILES, never their directories. `docs/verification/ui-ux-signoffs/` and
# `docs/verification/owned-outcome/` both keep receiving new documents, and a
# directory-wide entry would silently license a genuinely broken citation in the
# next one. Each line below is one file that already exists and already carries
# the findings.
#
# THE COST, stated rather than discovered: a NEW dangling citation added to one
# of these eight files is not caught. That is narrower than the alternative and
# it is the price of leaving frozen records alone.
#
# WHEN THIS SHOULD SHRINK: if a future decision rewrites or retires one of these
# documents, delete its line. An entry that suppresses nothing FAILS, so the
# checker will name it rather than let it sit.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CITATION_EXEMPT, third group — THE CEO'S WORKSPACE SPEC, BYTE-FOR-BYTE.
#
# Added 2026-09-11, re-synced 2026-09-12 when he added point 14.
