# WHAT IS NOT IN THIS FILE, AND WHY IT LEFT
#
# A fourth key, INSTANCE_MECHANISMS, used to sit here. It named an executable
# inside the private sibling repository — a one-off verification script that
# reads a public contract and is correctly not published — so that Check 4
# would not report it as a capability withheld from the customer.
#
# It made this gate mean two different things:
#
#   ON THE MACHINE THAT WROTE IT the private tree resolved, Check 4 ran, the
#   entry suppressed a real finding, and the gate was green.
#
#   IN A CLONE OF THIS REPOSITORY ALONE — which is every CI runner, every
#   contributor and every reader — no private tree resolves, so Check 4 never
#   ran, the entry therefore suppressed nothing, and the rule directly above
#   fired and turned the gate red over a file that is not in this repository.
#
# The rule was right both times. What was wrong was judging an exemption
# belonging to a check that had not run — and, secondarily, publishing the
# interior layout of a private repository in the one file the boundary contract
# is supposed to keep honest.
#
#       A VERDICT ON THIS TREE MAY NOT DEPEND ON WHAT ELSE IS ON THE DISK.
#
# So the excuse moved to where the thing it excuses lives. A private mechanism
# that is one operator's artifact rather than a withheld capability now carries
#
#       instance-mechanism: <why this is one operator's artifact>
#
# in its own body, in the private repository, reviewed in that repository's own
# diff — the discipline `dialect-exempt: <reason>` already uses here. A bare
# marker with no reason after it exempts nothing, and a marker on a file that
# couples to no public contract is itself reported as a stale exemption, so it
# cannot outlive its reason either.
#
# WHAT THAT LEAVES IS A PROPERTY, not a fixed instance: every key this file
# still accepts is a function of `git ls-files` and the tracked bytes, so its
# verdict is the same in every clone. INSTANCE_MECHANISMS is refused by name
# with the migration if it reappears; it is not quietly ignored.

